Notification outcome semantics
==============================

Fabric notify reports dispatched attempts separately from policy skips and
failed attempts. Any failed attempt makes the operation unsuccessful, while
preserving counts for partial success. This is an acknowledgement from the
configured provider or an in-app write, not proof of final recipient delivery.
An empty configuration continues to be an intentional no-op.

The shared helper preserves a failed result when a provider or in-app write
raises. Raw exception details, recipient addresses and provider error strings
are not written into these helper log messages or Fabric results. Lower-level
provider logs and stored provider responses have separate disclosure policies.
Cancellation propagates and does not trigger helper retry scheduling.
A failed in-app flush or commit raises a fixed persistence error and does not
publish a notification-created event. When commit=False, the enclosing caller
retains transaction ownership; publishing after a flush still does not guarantee
the outer transaction will later commit.

A retry can only refer to the delivery row assigned to the exact attempt after
its database flush succeeds. Missing providers, raised sends and failed
tracking flushes have no such identity; the helper does not borrow another
recipient's or organization's recent row with a matching subject. These failed
attempts remain visible to callers, but no automatic retry is promised for an
untracked attempt.

Limits requiring further operational qualification
--------------------------------------------------
A successful database flush is not a commit. This change does not introduce a
transactional outbox, guarantee delivery after a transaction rollback, or make
external provider sends exactly once. The existing shared-session concurrency
and enqueue-before-commit lifecycle still need dedicated transaction and broker
failure qualification. Successful channel sends are not undone if another
channel fails. Do not blindly retry an entire partially successful workflow.
Unknown channel names and configurations resolving no recipients retain their
existing behavior; configuration validation is a separate gate.

Regression checks use synthetic providers and do not send real notifications.
Customer SMTP/webhook/SMS acceptance requires explicit authorized destinations.

In-app recipient authorization
------------------------------
A non-null organization scope requires an active, non-deleted recipient in that
organization before any write or creation event. Missing, foreign, inactive or
deleted recipients return the same fixed error. Lookup errors refuse and
cancellation propagates. The trusted internal platform scope (organization_id
is None) retains its existing behavior; it is not a tenant authorization bypass
for Fabric, which supplies the Connection organization.
